Let's start by creating a test class that will verify the behavior of the `LogWithLevel` extension method, especially focusing on the call to `LogCritical` on line 41. We will mock `ILogger` and verify that the correct method is called with the expected parameters.

I'll prepare the test class with a test method that calls `LogWithLevel` with `LogLevel.Critical` and checks that `LogCritical` was invoked.

<tool>submit_test(csharp)</tool>
```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

namespace Volo.Abp.Core.Tests
{
    public class AbpLoggerExtensionsTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void LogWithLevel_Critical_ShouldCallLogCritical()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var message = "Critical message";

            // Act
            loggerMock.Object.LogWithLevel(LogLevel.Critical, message);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(x => x.LogCritical(message), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```

The verification failure indicates that Moq cannot handle extension method calls like `logger.LogCritical(message)` directly in `Verify`. To fix this, we need to set up the mock to intercept calls to the extension methods, which are actually calling the underlying `ILogger` methods like `Log(LogLevel, ...)`. 

In `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging`, extension methods like `LogCritical` internally call `ILogger.Log(LogLevel.Critical, ...)`. So, we should verify that `ILogger.Log` was called with the correct `LogLevel` and message.

I'll revise the test to verify that `ILogger.Log` was called with `LogLevel.Critical` and the message, instead of verifying `LogCritical` directly.

<tool>submit_test(csharp)</tool>
```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

namespace Volo.Abp.Core.Tests
{
    public class AbpLoggerExtensionsTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void LogWithLevel_Critical_ShouldCallLogWithCorrectLevelAndMessage()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var message = "Critical message";

            // Act
            loggerMock.Object.LogWithLevel(LogLevel.Critical, message);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(x => x.Log(
                LogLevel.Critical,
                It.IsAny<EventId>(),
                It.Is<It.IsAnyType>((v, t) => v.ToString() == message),
                null,
                It.IsAny<Func<It.IsAnyType, Exception, string>>()), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```
This verifies that the underlying `Log` method was called with `LogLevel.Critical` and the correct message. I'll submit this as the next version.
